 report=f'''# Radar-Native Discovery #5\n\nObserved {DATE}. RN5 screened 280 buyer markets and deep-scanned 24. The run originated from buyer decisions and external evidence, then checked Amazon—not the reverse.\n\n## Decision\n\n**RN5-001 — Solar Quote Decoder: CONDITIONAL GO — AWAITING HUMAN PRODUCT GATE.**\n\nThe thesis is narrow: a U.S. homeowner brings two to four real rooftop-solar or battery proposals and leaves with normalized cash/loan economics, production assumptions, equipment, scope, warranties, exclusions and unresolved questions. It is not a solar installation guide and does not recommend a loan or installer.\n\nThe underlying job is real. DOE tells buyers to compare quotes and cost per watt. CFPB documents hidden solar-loan markups and recommends multiple written proposals. EnergySage reports millions of marketplace data points and active quote demand. On Amazon, the exact workbook query produced only one tangential result, while recent adjacent homeowner solar guides showed visible traction. This is evidence of a proven problem with a weak exact book solution, though exact-workbook demand remains the main uncertainty.\n\nThe proposed transformation is: **buyer proposals → normalized cash/loan, equipment, assumptions, warranty and exclusion fields → an apples-to-apples sign-or-question decision packet.**\n\nFormat eligibility passes for English paperback. Kindle is supported but not commercially justified for a writable side-by-side workbook. Portfolio similarity is 8/100 and NOVEL against the complete known catalog.\n\nConservative hypothesis: $16.99 paperback, $6.50 royalty per unit, 30 units in 90 days, $195 EV, 1.5 Anton-hours, $130 per Anton-hour. BSR was not visible and was not invented.\n\nThe eight-page representative sample tests the transformation itself. Full production remains blocked until the human product gate passes.\n\n## RN4 to RN5\n\nRN4 concentrated too heavily on obvious Amazon keyword shelves and workbook concepts whose free alternatives or trust requirements erased paid value. Its gates behaved correctly. RN5 broadened discovery toward document-decoding, quote-normalization, cross-system and field-retrieval architectures, originated more candidates from external buyer problems, and applied format eligibility before economics. This reduced format waste and produced one conditional survivor without lowering any gate.\n\n## Stop condition\n\nDiscovery is complete. Production has not started.\n'''
 (OUT/'radar-native-5-report.md').write_text(report)
 learning='''# Radar-Native #5 learning\n\nRN4’s zero was primarily a combination of search-space concentration, obvious keyword markets and too many low-content/workbook concepts; the gates behaved appropriately. RN5 kept the gates and diversified the generating architecture.\n\n- Opportunity diversity: 280 markets versus 200, with 70 underlying buyer jobs and 14 architecture families rather than a mostly workbook-shaped shelf scan.\n- Gate survival: one of seven finalists reached conditional authorization; RN4 had zero of five.\n- Languages: all six requested languages were screened. EN and FR produced credible finalists; RU and BE were stopped as UNVERIFIED at format eligibility, and PL/UK candidates did not beat the evidence bar.\n- Discovery origin: RN5 deliberately included externally proven quote, report and decision problems whose exact book shelves were weak.\n- Format eligibility: it prevented RU/BE economics from being treated as publishable facts and kept Kindle out of a print-first winner’s thesis.\n- False positives: contractor-bid and used-EV ideas looked easy but current Amazon results exposed direct low-traction copycat competition; professional-authority risk blocked home-inspection elevation.\n- NO PRODUCT probability: still material. RN5 has only a conditional survivor; a failed human product gate converts the result to NO PRODUCT without lowering thresholds.\n\nDo not change permanent weights. After several launches, compare realized sales with `EXTERNALLY_PROVEN_JOB`, `EXACT_BOOK_GAP`, and `NEWCOMER_TRACTION_CONFIDENCE` before promoting them into scoring.\n'''
 (OUT/'radar-native-5-learning.md').write_text(learning)
